# Managed Sensors

## Overview

A sensor is a process that watches something outside the Agent, such as a
thermometer, a directory, or a price feed, and sends what it sees to the
Agent as Signals. `Jido.Plugin.SensorManager` lets an Agent own its sensors.
An Action returns which sensors should run, and the manager starts and stops
processes to match:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{:ok, state, [Jido.Plugin.SensorManager.start({:thermometer, "lab"}, MyApp.Thermometer, %{room: "lab"})]}
```

The list of desired sensors is Agent state. It commits with the Turn, so you
can test it with `cmd/2`. Sensors run under the Agent's activation: they
restart with it and stop with it.

In this Livebook you write a thermometer sensor, start and stop it from an
Action, and watch its readings arrive as Turns.

Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## Wait Helper

This helper polls a condition every 20 ms until it returns `{:ok, value}` or
3 seconds pass.

```elixir
defmodule SensorGuide.Wait do
  def until(fun, timeout \\ 3_000) do
    deadline = System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) + timeout
    loop(fun, deadline)
  end

  defp loop(fun, deadline) do
    case fun.() do
      {:ok, value} ->
        {:ok, value}

      _not_yet ->
        if System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) >= deadline do
          :timeout
        else
          Process.sleep(20)
          loop(fun, deadline)
        end
    end
  end
end
```

## Write A Sensor

A sensor is an OTP child process. It must:

- define `child_spec/1` (`use GenServer` provides one);
- accept one `Jido.Plugin.SensorManager.Init` struct when it starts; and
- send input with `Jido.AgentServer.cast(init.agent_server, signal)`.

This thermometer sends a fake reading every `interval_ms` ms:

```elixir
defmodule SensorGuide.Thermometer do
  use GenServer

  alias Jido.Plugin.SensorManager.Init

  def start_link(%Init{} = init), do: GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, init)

  @impl true
  def init(%Init{} = init) do
    send(self(), :read)
    {:ok, %{init: init, reading: 0}}
  end

  @impl true
  def handle_info(:read, state) do
    reading = state.reading + 1

    signal =
      Jido.Signal.new!(
        "temperature.reading",
        %{room: state.init.config.room, celsius: 20 + reading},
        source: "/sensors/thermometer"
      )

    Jido.AgentServer.cast(state.init.agent_server, signal)
    Process.send_after(self(), :read, state.init.config.interval_ms)
    {:noreply, %{state | reading: reading}}
  end
end
```

The `Init` struct fields:

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `agent_server` | PID of the owning Agent Server. |
| `agent_id` | ID of the owning Agent. |
| `tag` | This sensor's tag. |
| `module` | This sensor's module. |
| `config` | The config map passed to `start/3`. |
| `jido`, `partition` | The Agent's Jido instance and partition, when set. |

The sensor only reports readings. The Agent's routes decide what a reading
means.

## Define The Agent

The building Agent starts and stops one thermometer per room and stores the
latest reading for each:

```elixir
defmodule SensorGuide.Building do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "building"

  agent do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{readings: Zoi.map() |> Zoi.default(%{})}))
    plugin(Jido.Plugin.SensorManager)
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/managed-sensors")

    route "sensor.watch", as: :watch do
      action %{room: room}, schema: Zoi.object(%{room: Zoi.string()}), context: context do
        {:ok, context.agent_state,
         [
           Jido.Plugin.SensorManager.start(
             {:thermometer, room},
             SensorGuide.Thermometer,
             %{room: room, interval_ms: 100}
           )
         ]}
      end
    end

    route "sensor.unwatch", as: :unwatch do
      action %{room: room}, schema: Zoi.object(%{room: Zoi.string()}), context: context do
        {:ok, context.agent_state, [Jido.Plugin.SensorManager.stop({:thermometer, room})]}
      end
    end

    route "temperature.reading" do
      action %{room: room, celsius: celsius},
        schema: Zoi.object(%{room: Zoi.string(), celsius: Zoi.integer()}),
        context: context do
        readings = Map.put(context.agent_state.readings, room, celsius)
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | readings: readings}}
      end
    end
  end
end
```

The tag `{:thermometer, room}` identifies one sensor. Tags and config are
stored in Agent state, so they must be portable data: no PIDs, references, or
functions.

## Test The Decision

The Plugin adds a `sensors` field. Run the watch Signal with `cmd/2` to see
the change without starting a process:

```elixir
agent = SensorGuide.Building.instantiate!(id: "plan")
watch = SensorGuide.Building.watch_signal!(%{room: "lab"})
{:ok, candidate, [directive]} = SensorGuide.Building.cmd(agent, watch)

{agent.state.sensors, candidate.state.sensors, directive.__struct__}
```

The result is:

```text
{%{desired: %{}},
 %{desired: %{{:thermometer, "lab"} => %{module: SensorGuide.Thermometer,
                                         config: %{room: "lab", interval_ms: 100}}}},
 Jido.Plugin.SensorManager.Start}
```

No process started. The candidate lists one desired thermometer.

## Start The Building

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()
{:ok, building} = Jido.start_agent(SensorGuide.Building, id: "building")

Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(building)
```

The result is `:ok`. The manager's supervisor is running with no sensors.

## Start A Sensor

Send the same Signal to the live Agent:

```elixir
{:ok, committed, _directives} = Jido.AgentServer.call(building, watch)

Map.keys(committed.state.sensors.desired)
```

The result is `[thermometer: "lab"]`: one tag, `{:thermometer, "lab"}`,
which Elixir prints as a keyword list.

The Server commits the new desired set first. Then the manager handles the
`Start` Directive: it starts each desired sensor that isn't running and stops
each running sensor that isn't desired.

Wait for readings:

```elixir
SensorGuide.Wait.until(fn ->
  case Jido.AgentServer.agent(building).state.readings do
    %{"lab" => celsius} when celsius >= 22 -> {:ok, :reading}
    _ -> :wait
  end
end)
```

The result is `{:ok, :reading}`. Each reading is cast to the Agent, routed,
and committed by a normal Turn.

## Stop A Sensor

```elixir
unwatch = SensorGuide.Building.unwatch_signal!(%{room: "lab"})
{:ok, committed, _directives} = Jido.AgentServer.call(building, unwatch)

committed.state.sensors
```

The result is `%{desired: %{}}`. After the commit, the manager stops the lab
thermometer, and readings stop changing:

```elixir
Process.sleep(150)
before = Jido.AgentServer.agent(building).state.readings["lab"]
Process.sleep(300)

before == Jido.AgentServer.agent(building).state.readings["lab"]
```

The result is `true`. The last reading stays in state.

## Replace A Sensor

Calling `start/3` with a tag that is already running:

- with a different module or config, stops the old process and starts a new
  one;
- with the same module and config, leaves the running process alone.

You can return the same `start/3` Directive on every Turn without restarting
the sensor.

## Restarts

The manager supervises sensors inside the Agent's activation:

| Event | Result |
| --- | --- |
| A sensor crashes | The manager restarts it at once while its tag is desired. |
| A sensor fails to start | The manager retries after `retry_delay_ms`. |
| The Agent Server crashes | The activation restarts, and the manager starts every sensor in `sensors.desired`. |
| The Agent stops | Every sensor stops. |

A crash restart has no backoff and no limit. Sensors run as `:temporary`
children, and the manager restarts them itself when it sees the exit, so no
supervisor restart limit applies. A sensor that crashes right after it starts
restarts in a loop. Guard against this inside the sensor, for example by
catching errors in `handle_info/2`.

Each reconcile carries the revision that produced it. The manager skips any
revision at or below the last one it applied, so a delayed retry can't
replace a newer sensor set.

A restarted sensor starts with fresh process state. The thermometer's
`reading` counter resets to 0. Keep anything a sensor must remember in the
Agent, by sending it as a Signal, or in an external store.

## Plugin Options

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `retry_delay_ms` | `1_000` | Delay before retrying a failed reconcile. Integer from 1 to 4,294,967,295. |

An invalid value fails Agent startup before any sensor starts.

## Stop The Example

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent("building")
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

## Summary

- A sensor is an OTP child that casts Signals to its Agent.
- `SensorManager.start/3` and `stop/1` are Directives. They change the
  desired set, and the manager starts or stops processes after commit.
- `agent.state.sensors.desired` holds the desired set, so `cmd/2` can test it.
- Sensors restart while desired, recover with the Agent, and stop with it.
- Sensor process state doesn't survive a restart. Agent state does.

